    title: "The Civil Rights Movement",
    topic: "History",
    content: "The Civil Rights Movement was one of the most important social and political struggles in American history. It took place mainly during the 1950s and 1960s, when African Americans fought peacefully for equal rights and an end to racial segregation. Although the movement involved millions of people, a few key figures became symbols of courage and change.\n\nRosa Parks is often called the \"mother of the civil rights movement.\" In 1955, she refused to give up her seat on a bus in Montgomery, Alabama, to a white passenger. Her act of quiet defiance sparked the Montgomery Bus Boycott, during which African Americans refused to ride city buses for 381 days. The boycott was a powerful demonstration of economic and social protest.\n\nDr. Martin Luther King Jr. emerged as the movement's most prominent leader. He had been studying the nonviolent protest methods of Mahatma Gandhi and believed that peaceful resistance was the most effective strategy. In 1963, he delivered his famous \"I Have a Dream\" speech in Washington, D.C., before a crowd of more than 250,000 people. His words described a vision of a nation where people would be judged not by the color of their skin, but by the content of their character.\n\nThe movement achieved significant legislative victories. The Civil Rights Act of 1964 outlawed discrimination based on race, color, religion, or national origin. The Voting Rights Act of 1965 removed barriers that had prevented African Americans from voting. These laws transformed American society, though the struggle for true equality has continued long after those landmark victories.\n\nThe legacy of the Civil Rights Movement extends far beyond the United States. It inspired people around the world who were fighting against injustice and oppression. The movement demonstrated that ordinary citizens, when organized and determined, have the power to change laws and transform a nation. It remains one of history's most powerful examples of peaceful resistance making a lasting difference.",
    content_zh: "公民權利運動是美國歷史上最重要的社會與政治鬥爭之一。它主要發生在1950年代至1960年代，當時非裔美國人為了爭取平等權利及終結種族隔離制度而進行和平抗爭。儘管這場運動涉及數百萬人，少數關鍵人物成為了勇氣與改變的象徵。\n\n羅莎·帕克斯常被稱為「公民權利運動之母」。1955年，她拒絕在阿拉巴馬州蒙哥馬利市的公車上將座位讓給白人乘客。她這一無聲的反抗行動引發了蒙哥馬利公車抵制運動，在381天內非裔美國人拒絕搭乘城市公車。這場抵制活動是一次有力的經濟與社會抗議示範。\n\n馬丁·路德·金恩博士成為這場運動最著名的領袖。他曾研究聖雄甘地的非暴力抗議方式，相信和平抵抗是最有效的策略。1963年，他在華盛頓特區向超過25萬人發表了著名的「我有一個夢想」演講。他的話語描述了一個人們不以膚色、而以品格內涵相互評判的國家願景。\n\n這場運動取得了重大的立法勝利。1964年的《民權法案》禁止基於種族、膚色、宗教或國籍的歧視。1965年的《投票權法案》消除了阻礙非裔美國人投票的障礙。這些法律改變了美國社會，儘管真正平等的鬥爭在這些里程碑式的勝利之後仍持續很久。\n\n公民權利運動的遺產遠遠超出了美國範疇。它激勵了世界各地正在對抗不公與壓迫的人們。這場運動證明，普通公民只要有組織且堅定，就有能力改變法律、改變國家。它至今仍是歷史上和平抵抗帶來持久改變最有力的範例之一。",
